The 8-Hours-a-Week AI Engineer Roadmap (2026–2028)
How to Spend Your 8 Hours Every Week
This split matters more than you think. Stick to it:

5 hours — Building (projects, code, hands-on)
2 hours — Learning (courses, docs, papers, videos)
1 hour — Reading the field (newsletters, Twitter/X, blogs, release notes)

The 1-hour "reading the field" habit is non-negotiable. AI moves fast. Engineers who don't track it become obsolete in 18 months. Subscribe to: The Batch (Andrew Ng), Latent Space, Simon Willison's blog, and follow Anthropic/OpenAI release notes.

PHASE 1 — Python & Dev Fundamentals (Weeks 1–6, ~48 hours)
You said you know some Python. We're upgrading that to professional Python, because sloppy code is the #1 reason junior AI engineers get filtered out.
What to learn:

Python deep: type hints, dataclasses, async/await, context managers, decorators, virtual environments (uv is the modern tool, skip venv/conda)
Git & GitHub: branches, PRs, merge conflicts, writing good commit messages
Command line comfort: bash basics, environment variables, SSH
HTTP & APIs: REST, status codes, JSON, authentication headers
One real skill: building and calling APIs with httpx or requests

Resources (pick one, don't shop around):

Python Crash Course (3rd ed.) by Eric Matthes — chapters 1-11, skip the games
Corey Schafer's Python YouTube playlist for gaps
git — "Learn Git Branching" (interactive, free, 2 hours)

Project (Week 5–6): Build a CLI tool that takes a URL, fetches the page, and saves a cleaned version to disk. Push it to GitHub with a real README. This is your first portfolio piece.
Skip: Leetcode. Data structures deep-dives. Django. Flask. You don't need them yet.

PHASE 2 — LLM Fundamentals & Your First AI App (Weeks 7–14, ~64 hours)
This is where you start being an AI engineer. Notice I skipped "learn ML from scratch." At 8 hrs/week, you cannot afford a 3-month detour into gradient descent. You'll pick up ML intuition along the way.
What to learn:

How LLMs actually work at a conceptual level (tokens, context windows, temperature, top-p, sampling) — not the math, the mental model
The Anthropic API and/or OpenAI API — reading the docs cover to cover is genuinely worth it
Prompt engineering that works: system prompts, few-shot, structured output, XML vs JSON prompting
Tool use / function calling — this is the foundation of agents
Streaming responses and handling errors
Token counting and cost management

Resources:

Anthropic's prompt engineering docs (the official ones at docs.claude.com)
Karpathy's "Intro to LLMs" YouTube video (1 hour, watch twice)
Karpathy's "Let's build GPT from scratch" — watch it once for intuition, don't try to replicate
Building LLM Apps course by DeepLearning.AI (the short courses, each ~1 hour)

Projects (ship 2):

Weeks 9–11: A CLI chatbot with conversation memory and streaming. Add a system prompt that gives it a real purpose (study buddy, code reviewer, whatever).
Weeks 12–14: A structured-output app — feed it messy input (emails, receipts, meeting notes), get clean JSON out. This shows you understand production LLM work.

Skip: LangChain for now. Seriously. Learn the raw APIs first — frameworks make sense only after you understand what they're abstracting.

PHASE 3 — RAG: The Skill That Pays Rent (Weeks 15–24, ~80 hours)
Retrieval-Augmented Generation is the single most employable skill right now. Nearly every company wants "chat with our docs." Master this phase and you can start freelancing by month 6.
What to learn:

Embeddings: what they are, how to generate them, how similarity search works
Chunking strategies (this is where most RAG systems fail — chunking is an art)
Vector databases: start with Chroma (local, free), then learn Pinecone or Qdrant
Hybrid search: combining keyword (BM25) + semantic search
Reranking with cross-encoders
Context window management and the "lost in the middle" problem
Evaluation: how do you actually know if your RAG is good? (Hint: this is what separates junior from mid-level)

Resources:

"RAG From Scratch" series by Langchain on YouTube (watch for concepts, not the framework)
Jason Liu's writing on RAG (he's the best practical source alive)
Anthropic's Contextual Retrieval blog post

Project (Weeks 20–24): Build "Chat with your PDFs" — but make it good. Handle multi-document queries, cite sources with page numbers, add a simple eval set of 20 questions where you measure accuracy. Deploy it as a web app (Streamlit is fine for now). This project alone, done well, can get you freelance work.

PHASE 4 — Agents & Tool Use (Weeks 25–36, ~96 hours)
Agents are the 2028 play. By then, "build an agent for X" will be as common as "build a website for X" is today. The engineers who master agent reliability now will be extremely valuable.
What to learn:

The agent loop: plan → act → observe → repeat
Tool/function calling at a deep level — schemas, error handling, parallel tool calls
Multi-step reasoning, self-correction, and when to let the model decide vs constrain it
Agent frameworks: try Anthropic's Claude Agent SDK and/or OpenAI's Agents SDK
MCP (Model Context Protocol) — this will matter a lot by 2028
Handling failures: retries, fallbacks, guardrails
Cost/latency tradeoffs in multi-step agents

Resources:

Anthropic's "Building Effective Agents" blog post (read it 3 times)
The Claude Agent SDK docs
Simon Willison's blog posts on agents (brutally practical)

Projects (ship 2):

Weeks 28–31: A research agent — give it a topic, it searches the web, synthesizes 3+ sources, produces a report with citations.
Weeks 32–36: A "real job" agent — pick something annoying in your actual life (processing invoices, sorting emails, monitoring prices) and build an agent that does it autonomously. This project becomes your interview showcase.


PHASE 5 — Production, Evals & Deployment (Weeks 37–48, ~96 hours)
This is the phase that converts you from "can build demos" to "can ship software." Most self-taught AI people skip this. Don't.
What to learn:

FastAPI for building real AI backends
Docker basics (enough to containerize your app)
One cloud: pick AWS, GCP, or Railway/Render for simplicity. Don't learn all three.
Observability: logging LLM calls, tracking costs, tracing multi-step flows (LangSmith, Langfuse, or roll your own)
Evaluations — this is the core skill of senior AI engineers. How do you prove your AI works? LLM-as-judge, golden datasets, regression testing
Security: prompt injection, data leakage, API key management
Streaming in production, handling long-running tasks, background jobs

