YEAR 2 — Depth, Portfolio, and Getting Hired (Weeks 49–104, ~400 hours)
Year 1 made you competent. Year 2 makes you employable.
Weeks 49–64 — Pick a depth area and go deep. Options:

Agent systems (if you loved Phase 4) — multi-agent, long-running agents, computer use
RAG at scale — enterprise search, structured + unstructured data, graph RAG
Fine-tuning — LoRA, distillation, when to fine-tune vs prompt (usually prompt wins; knowing when it doesn't is valuable)
Voice/multimodal — this is a sleeper area for 2028

Weeks 65–80 — Build two portfolio-grade projects. Not tutorials. Not toys. Real things that solve real problems, with proper evals, deployment, and writeups. These are your job interviews.
Weeks 81–92 — Go public.

Write 5 technical blog posts (one per month). Topics: lessons from your projects, deep dives into things you learned, opinions on industry debates
Contribute to an open source AI project (even tiny PRs to docs count)
Post your projects on X, Hacker News, relevant subreddits
Start a GitHub that screams "hire me"

Weeks 93–104 — Job search.

Apply to 5 roles a week, not 50. Quality beats volume.
Target: "AI Engineer," "ML Engineer (Applied)," "Forward Deployed Engineer," "AI Developer." Avoid "ML Research" and "Data Scientist" unless you do extra math.
Use your projects as interview material. "Walk me through a project" is where you win.
Also look at AI-forward startups — they hire on demonstrated ability over credentials more than big tech does.


The Hard Truths
A few things nobody will tell you but I will:
8 hrs/week is the floor, not the target. If you can find a 9th or 10th hour on weekends, take it. The difference between 8 and 12 hrs/week is the difference between "employable in 2 years" and "employable in 15 months."
Consistency beats intensity. One hour every day beats 8 hours on Sunday. Your brain needs sleep to consolidate this stuff.
You will feel behind. Ignore that feeling. In 2026, everyone on Twitter seems to already know everything. They don't. They're performing. Keep your head down.
Build in public from month 3. Even bad projects, posted publicly with honest writeups, build a reputation. Most people wait until their work is "ready." It never is.
By 2028, "I used ChatGPT to help me build this" is table stakes. Using Claude Code / Cursor / Copilot aggressively is expected. The engineers who ship fast with AI tools will out-earn the ones who don't — start practicing this from Phase 2 onward.
